# docker/Dockerfile ends with `COPY . .`, so whatever sits in this directory at
# build time is what ships in the image. Without this file that includes what
# git deliberately hides -- and `git status` will never mention those, because
# not mentioning them is its job.

# Secrets, and not merely as dead weight: internal/config/env.go calls
# godotenv.Load() during startup, which reads /app/.env. A developer's local
# credentials copied into the image would not sit there inertly; the running
# container would load them as its own configuration.
.env
.env.*

# Installed inside the image by `RUN npm install`, which runs *before* the
# COPY. Copying a host node_modules over it replaces packages built for Linux
# with packages built for macOS, native binaries included.
node_modules/

# Produced by `task build` during the image build. A stale copy from the host
# is at best ignored and at worst a binary for the wrong operating system.
dist/
.task/

# Nothing in the build reads git history: set-version.sh stamps the version
# into the source instead. The directory also holds whatever credential the
# checkout left in .git/config.
.git/

# Not part of the application. Only internal/view/static is embedded into the
# binary; docs/ deploys separately, and assets/ is imagery for the README.
#
# .devcontainer/ is deliberately NOT here: docker/Dockerfile.dev line 100 does
# `COPY .devcontainer/.bashrc /root/.bashrc`, and that is the image CI builds to
# run lint, test and build in. Excluding it costs a few kilobytes in the
# production image and breaks every CI run.
docs/
assets/
.github/
.vscode/
.idea/

# Local tooling configuration. These are untracked rather than ignored, so they
# are the easy ones to forget: git shows them, nobody reads them, and they ship.
.mcp.json
.claude/

# Temporary and test output.
tmp/
temp/
*.test
*.out
